1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,212
Chapter 35: Stillbirth.

3
00:00:05,212 --> 00:00:22,262
Definitions Etiologies Risk Factors Evaluation Of The Stillborn Fetus Perinatal Care Prior Stillbirth Changes In Stillbirth Rates References Perinatal mortality combines fetal and neonatal deaths and is one measure of healthcare quality before, during, and after delivery.

4
00:00:22,262 --> 00:00:30,387
Fetal mortality data from the National Vital Statistics system are usually presented for fetal deaths after the 20-week threshold.

5
00:00:30,387 --> 00:00:41,637
Using this threshold, the numbers of fetal deaths in the United States in 2013 slightly surpassed numbers of neonatal deaths when considering fetal deaths beyond 20 weeks .

6
00:00:41,637 --> 00:00:44,212
Figure 35-1.

7
00:00:44,212 --> 00:00:50,062
Distribution of fetal and neonatal deaths in the United States, 2013.

8
00:00:50,062 --> 00:00:53,450
Data from MacDorman, 2015a.

9
00:00:53,450 --> 00:00:55,300
Definitions.

10
00:00:55,300 --> 00:01:07,912
As discussed in Chapter 1 (p. 3), the current definition of fetal death adopted in the United States by the National Center for Health Statistics is based on one recommended by the World Health Organization .

11
00:01:07,912 --> 00:01:20,550
It states that “Fetal death means death prior to complete expulsion or extraction from the mother of a product of human conception irrespective of the duration of pregnancy and which is not an induced termination of pregnancy.

12
00:01:20,550 --> 00:01:35,112
The death is indicated by the fact that after such expulsion or extraction, the fetus does not breathe or show any other evidence of life such as beating of the heart, pulsation of the umbilical cord, or definite movement of voluntary muscles.

13
00:01:35,112 --> 00:01:44,537
Heartbeats are to be distinguished from transient cardiac contractions; respirations are to be distinguished from fleeting respiratory efforts or gasps.

14
00:01:44,537 --> 00:01:51,937
Reporting requirements for fetal deaths in the United States are determined by each state, and thus criteria differ.

15
00:01:51,937 --> 00:02:04,850
Most states mandate reporting of deaths of fetuses that are 20 weeks’ gestation or older or have a minimum birthweight of 350 g, which roughly equates to that at 20 weeks— or a combination of both.

16
00:02:04,850 --> 00:02:12,450
However, evidence shows that not all required fetal deaths are reported, especially at earlier gestational ages .

17
00:02:12,450 --> 00:02:18,637
Similarly, comparisons of rates among countries are limited by incomplete fetal death data.

18
00:02:18,637 --> 00:02:24,862
Internationally, less than 5 percent of neonatal deaths have formalized documentation .

19
00:02:24,862 --> 00:02:32,300
Further, comparative analyses using birthweight versus gestational age among countries yield discordant results.

20
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:44,437
In the United States, if stillbirth were defined by a birthweight ≥500 g, the stillbirth rate would be reduced by 40 percent compared with a 20-week-age defined cohort .

21
00:02:44,437 --> 00:02:48,500
Three fetal mortality epochs are generally recognized.

22
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:59,525
Early describes deaths before 20 completed weeks’ gestation; intermediate for those between 20 and 27 weeks; and late for those ≥28 weeks.

23
00:02:59,525 --> 00:03:04,850
The fetal mortality rate in each epoch has changed little since 2006.

24
00:03:04,850 --> 00:03:14,700
As shown in Figure 35-2, fetal death rates are highest at the earliest and latest gestational ages, which suggests etiological differences.

25
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:17,225
Figure 35-2.

26
00:03:17,225 --> 00:03:24,112
National Vital Statistics Report: fetal mortality rate per 1000 births by weeks’ gestation.

27
00:03:24,112 --> 00:03:27,500
Data from MacDorman, 2015a.

28
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:29,437
Etiologies.

29
00:03:29,437 --> 00:03:46,587
To ascertain stillbirth causes in racially and geographically diverse populations in the United States, the Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (NICHD) organized the Stillbirth Collaborative Research Network Writing Group (2011b).

30
00:03:46,587 --> 00:03:57,700
The group examined reasons for death at 20 weeks’ gestation or later between 2006 and 2008 in 59 tertiary care and community hospitals in five states.

31
00:03:57,700 --> 00:04:08,350
Standardized evaluations included autopsy, placental histology, and testing of maternal or fetal blood/tissues, including fetal karyotyping.

32
00:04:08,350 --> 00:04:13,862
Evaluations were performed in 500 women with 512 stillbirths.

33
00:04:13,862 --> 00:04:17,775
Of these losses, 83 percent were before labor.

34
00:04:17,775 --> 00:04:21,112
Causes were divided into eight categories .

35
00:04:21,112 --> 00:04:26,975
These categories were then classified as probable, possible, or unknown causes.

36
00:04:26,975 --> 00:04:37,612
As an example, diabetes was considered a probable cause if the fetus had diabetic embryopathy with lethal anomalies or if the mother had diabetic ketoacidosis.

37
00:04:37,612 --> 00:04:43,400
It was a possible cause if the mother had poor glycemic control and the fetus had abnormal

38
00:04:43,564 --> 00:04:45,026
growth.

39
00:04:44,976 --> 00:04:50,501
A probable or possible source was identified in 76 percent of cases.

40
00:04:50,501 --> 00:04:56,126
This proportion is similar to that from Australia using a comparable national audit tool .

41
00:04:56,126 --> 00:04:58,689
Table 35-1.

42
00:04:58,689 --> 00:05:08,764
Causes of 512 Stillbirths in the Stillbirth Collaborative Research Network Study This Network study is unprecedented in the United States for several reasons.

43
00:05:08,764 --> 00:05:15,814
It was a population-based cohort of stillbirths, in which all underwent systematic and thorough evaluation.

44
00:05:15,814 --> 00:05:23,276
Each assigned cause of fetal death is reasonably straightforward and comprehensible except for “placental abnormalities.

45
00:05:23,276 --> 00:05:30,564
This category contains uteroplacental insufficiency and a few other less clearly defined placental entities.

46
00:05:30,564 --> 00:05:43,289
This aside, the leading reasons for fetal death were obstetrical and primarily included abruption, multifetal gestation complications, and spontaneous labor or ruptured membranes before viability.

47
00:05:43,289 --> 00:05:45,926
Structural anomaly was another .

48
00:05:45,926 --> 00:05:54,401
This study also illustrated that systematic evaluation may identify a likely cause in approximately three fourths of stillbirths.

49
00:05:54,401 --> 00:05:59,301
This high rate emphasizes the importance of standardized evaluation.

50
00:05:59,301 --> 00:06:09,351
However, a Cochrane database study concluded that evidence specifically from randomized trials to support specific tests for stillbirth evaluation was lacking .

51
00:06:09,351 --> 00:06:11,314
Risk Factors.

52
00:06:11,314 --> 00:06:15,826
Many factors are associated with an increased stillbirth risk .

53
00:06:15,826 --> 00:06:29,864
Major associations include sociodemographic factors such as non-Hispanic black women, who have higher incidences of hypertension, diabetes, placental abruption, and preterm premature rupture of membranes .

54
00:06:29,864 --> 00:06:46,551
Other common associations are maternal age at either end of the spectrum, obesity, preeclampsia, diabetes, substance use, and late-term pregnancy (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020; Page, 2018).

55
00:06:46,551 --> 00:06:52,301
Multifetal pregnancies, especially monochorionic gestations, are high risk .

56
00:06:52,301 --> 00:07:04,751
Women with prior adverse pregnancy outcomes—such as stillbirth, preterm birth, or growth restriction—also carry an increased risk (Reddy, 2010; Varner, 2014).

57
00:07:04,751 --> 00:07:21,639
Pregnancies complicated by current fetal-growth restriction are particularly vulnerable because of their associations with hypertension, diabetes, fetal infection, autoimmune disorders, and chronic renal disease (Brackett, 2020; Silver, 2018).

58
00:07:21,639 --> 00:07:29,714
Recently, the SARS-CoV-2 pandemic has posed both direct and indirect risks for adverse perinatal outcomes.

59
00:07:29,714 --> 00:07:42,526
Data are mixed as to whether rates of stillbirth have been higher during the pandemic (Khalil, 2020; Srivastava, 2021; Simon, 2021; Son, 2021b).

60
00:07:42,526 --> 00:07:46,264
Some suggest greater risks in low-source countries .

61
00:07:46,264 --> 00:07:57,676
Although with recognized limitations, these findings merit further investigation regarding perinatal care during this pandemic (Hu, 2021; Rasmussen, 2020).

62
00:07:57,676 --> 00:08:09,364
In 245 gravidas with SARS-CoV-2 infection at Parkland Hospital, the stillbirth rate was not increased compared with that in gravidas with negative test results during the same period.

63
00:08:09,364 --> 00:08:11,914
Table 35-2.

64
00:08:11,914 --> 00:08:24,126
Maternal Risk Factors and Estimated Stillbirth Rate To help lower stillbirth rates, two major studies assessed whether risk factors could be identified either before or shortly after pregnancy confirmation.

65
00:08:24,126 --> 00:08:31,326
In the first, Reddy and colleagues (2010) analyzed data from the NICHD Consortium on Safe Labor.

66
00:08:31,326 --> 00:08:43,114
Briefly, the pregnancy outcomes of 206,969 women delivered between 2002 and 2008 at 19 hospitals in the United States were analyzed.

67
00:08:43,114 --> 00:08:49,401
When stillbirths were apportioned according to gestational age, most occurred primarily at term.

68
00:08:49,401 --> 00:08:57,576
These investigators concluded that their results did not support routine antenatal surveillance for any demographic risk factors.

69
00:08:57,576 --> 00:09:06,176
The second analysis of risk factors was included in the Stillbirth Collaborative Research Network study described earlier (2011a).

70
00:09:06,176 --> 00:09:12,339
The validity of stillbirth prediction was assessed based on risks identified in early pregnancy.

71
00:09:12,339 --> 00:09:19,526
They found that pregnancy factors known at the start of pregnancy accounted for only a small proportion of stillbirth risk.

72
00:09:19,526 --> 00:09:29,364
Indeed, except for prior stillbirth or pregnancy loss from causes such as preterm birth or fetal-growth restriction, other risks had limited predictive value.

73
00:09:29,524 --> 00:09:38,261
Prior stillbirth as a risk for recurrence has been emphasized by others (Lamont, 2015; Sharma, 2006).

74
00:09:38,211 --> 00:09:43,961
Specifically, the stillbirth risk was fivefold higher in women with a prior stillbirth.

75
00:09:43,961 --> 00:09:54,286
From another report, prior preterm birth, fetalgrowth restriction, preeclampsia, and placental abruption were strongly associated with subsequent stillbirth .

76
00:09:54,286 --> 00:09:57,336
Evaluation Of The Stillborn Fetus.

77
00:09:57,336 --> 00:10:00,199
# Clinical Examination.

78
00:10:00,199 --> 00:10:12,849
Determining the cause of fetal death aids maternal coping, helps assuage perceived guilt, permits more accurate counseling regarding recurrence risk, and may prompt intervention to prevent a recurrent outcome.

79
00:10:12,849 --> 00:10:17,911
Identification of inherited syndromes also assists other family members.

80
00:10:17,911 --> 00:10:27,961
Important tests include autopsy, chromosomal analysis, and pathological examination of the placenta, cord, and chorioamnionic membranes .

81
00:10:27,961 --> 00:10:35,611
Page and coworkers (2017) found placental pathology and fetal autopsy to be the most useful.

82
00:10:35,611 --> 00:10:41,474
Clinically, the fetal weight, head circumference, and length and the placental weight are measured .

83
00:10:41,474 --> 00:10:45,211
Photographs are taken of the extremities and palms.

84
00:10:45,211 --> 00:10:50,249
Frontal plus profile views of the whole fetus and face also are obtained.

85
00:10:50,249 --> 00:10:54,986
A full radiograph of the fetus—a fetogram—may be performed .

86
00:10:54,986 --> 00:11:08,511
Postnatal magnetic resonance (MR) imaging, radiographs, or sonography may be especially important if parents decline a full autopsy (McPherson, 2017; Shruthi, 2018).

87
00:11:08,511 --> 00:11:15,261
Findings are documented in the medical record, and relevant ante- and intrapartum events are delineated.

88
00:11:15,261 --> 00:11:17,886
Figure 35-3.

89
00:11:17,886 --> 00:11:20,974
Flow chart for stillbirth evaluation.

90
00:11:20,974 --> 00:11:32,536
APAS = antiphospholipid antibody syndrome; KB = Kleihauer-Betke; MR = magnetic resonance; SLE = systemic lupus erythematosus.

91
00:11:32,536 --> 00:11:40,986
From American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020; Page, 2017, 2018.

92
00:11:40,986 --> 00:11:44,624
The reported incidence of fetal anomalies varies.

93
00:11:44,624 --> 00:11:58,499
For example, FayePetersen and colleagues (1999) reported that if autopsy and chromosomal studies are performed, up to 35 percent of stillborn fetuses were discovered to have major structural anomalies.

94
00:11:58,499 --> 00:12:10,749
Approximately 20 percent had dysmorphic features or skeletal anomalies, and 8 percent had chromosomal abnormalities (Pauli, 1994; Saller, 1995).

95
00:12:10,749 --> 00:12:22,224
As shown in Table 35-1, however, the incidence of structural anomalies in the Stillbirth Collaborative Research Network (2011b) was only 14 percent.

96
00:12:22,224 --> 00:12:25,286
# Laboratory Evaluation.

97
00:12:25,286 --> 00:12:33,424
In the absence of anatomical dysmorphology, up to 5 percent of stillborn fetuses will have a chromosomal abnormality .

98
00:12:33,424 --> 00:12:54,686
Previously, karyotyping all stillborn fetuses was standard practice, but whole-genome sequencing—such as with chromosomal microarray analysis (CMA)—is now recommended for stillborns (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020; Holmes, 2018; Martinez-Portilla, 2019).

99
00:12:54,686 --> 00:13:02,136
Described further in Chapter 16 (p. 325), this technique does not require dividing cells.

100
00:13:02,136 --> 00:13:08,411
This is especially helpful because culturing of macerated fetal tissue is frequently unsuccessful .

101
00:13:08,411 --> 00:13:21,386
Looking to the future, Stanley and associates (2020) evaluated exome sequencing in 246 unexplained stillbirths to search for disease-causing variants in the coding region of the genome.

102
00:13:21,386 --> 00:13:33,574
These investigators found that 8.5 percent of stillbirths with a normal chromosomal microarray and without probable maternal or obstetrical causes were probably attributable to mendelian disorders.

103
00:13:33,574 --> 00:13:43,111
These findings suggest that a portion of stillbirth cases is caused by pathological variants in genes known to underlie disorders in infants and adults.

104
00:13:43,111 --> 00:13:53,886
In addition, a similar number of cases are caused by lossof-function variants in genes that are critical to in-utero survival but not currently known to be associated with stillbirth.

105
00:13:53,886 --> 00:14:03,074
Thus, the full phenotypic spectrum of many known mendelian disorders is not fully understood without the inclusion of cases resulting in stillbirth .

106
00:14:03,074 --> 00:14:08,399
To take fetal samples, a provider must obtain appropriate parental consent.

107
00:14:08,399 --> 00:14:15,649
Any type of fetal or placental tissue or amnionic fluid can be submitted for genetic testing using CMA .

108
00:14:15,649 --> 00:14:20,099
Contamination with maternal tissue or blood is ideally avoided.

109
00:14:20,099 --> 00:14:24,311
Specimens are collected by sterile technique and kept at room

110
00:14:24,460 --> 00:14:26,085
temperature.

111
00:14:26,035 --> 00:14:51,285
If fetal blood cannot be withdrawn from the umbilical cord or heart, at least one of the following can be sent for testing: (1) placental block measuring 1 × 1 cm taken below the cord insertion site in a specimen not fixed in formalin; (2) 1.5-cm-long umbilical cord segment; or (3) internal fetal tissue sample from the costochondral junction, fascia lata, or patella.

112
00:14:51,285 --> 00:14:58,097
Tissue is washed with sterile saline and suspended in Ringer solution, or sterile cytogenetic medium.

113
00:14:58,097 --> 00:15:01,585
Formalin or alcohol kills viable cells.

114
00:15:01,585 --> 00:15:10,297
If conventional karyotyping is the only test available and the death is recent, amnionic fluid can be obtained by amniocentesis.

115
00:15:10,297 --> 00:15:15,160
This is particularly valuable if delivery is not expected imminently.

116
00:15:15,160 --> 00:15:23,410
As such, these cells provide a greater likelihood of cell growth and eventual results compared with tissue harvested after delivery.

117
00:15:23,410 --> 00:15:36,185
Maternal blood is obtained for Kleihauer-Betke staining; for antiphospholipid antibody and lupus anticoagulant testing if indicated; and for serum glucose measurement to exclude overt diabetes .

118
00:15:36,185 --> 00:15:51,110
Testing for syphilis is reasonable if no prior screen has been performed, if the patient has exposure risk factors, or if patient, fetus, or placenta show suggestive findings, described in Chapter 68 (p. 1208) .

119
00:15:51,110 --> 00:16:01,347
Although some have recommended routine evaluation of heritable thrombophilias, no evidence supports the clinical efficiency of screening in an unselected population.

120
00:16:01,347 --> 00:16:11,510
Silver and colleagues (2016) found that most maternal and fetal thrombophilias were not associated with stillbirth and recommended against routine testing.

121
00:16:11,510 --> 00:16:20,060
This is echoed by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the Society for MaternalFetal Medicine (2020).

122
00:16:20,060 --> 00:16:22,972
# Placental Examination.

123
00:16:22,972 --> 00:16:30,772
Gross and microscopical examination of the placenta, membranes, and cord by a perinatal pathologist is ideal.

124
00:16:30,772 --> 00:16:34,885
Numerous maternal and fetal disorders can be identified.

125
00:16:34,885 --> 00:16:42,997
Cord knotting and placental microvascular abnormalities are examples and discussed in Chapter 6 (p. 107).

126
00:16:42,997 --> 00:16:48,247
Implicated infections are described in Chapters 67 and 68.

127
00:16:48,247 --> 00:16:50,510
# Autopsy.

128
00:16:50,510 --> 00:16:56,710
Parents are encouraged to allow a full autopsy, which is more likely to yield valuable data.

129
00:16:56,710 --> 00:17:04,897
Pinar and coworkers (2012) provide the autopsy protocol used by the Stillbirth Collaborative Research Network.

130
00:17:04,897 --> 00:17:16,297
An analysis of 400 consecutive fetal deaths in Wales showed that autopsy altered the presumed cause of death in 13 percent and provided new data in another 26 percent .

131
00:17:16,297 --> 00:17:30,135
Other investigators have found that autopsy results changed the recurrence risk estimates and parental counseling in 25 to 50 percent of cases (Faye-Petersen, 1999; Silver, 2007).

132
00:17:30,135 --> 00:17:40,322
Miller and associates (2016) reported that placental examination with autopsy altered future medical management in 45 percent of cases.

133
00:17:40,322 --> 00:17:45,147
Limited autopsy protocols can also yield valuable information.

134
00:17:45,147 --> 00:17:58,597
Of components, external examination combined with photography, radiography, MR imaging, bacterial cultures, and selective use of chromosomal and histopathological studies often aids determination.

135
00:17:58,597 --> 00:18:03,460
According to one survey, most hospitals do not audit stillbirths .

136
00:18:03,460 --> 00:18:18,985
In other centers, including ours, maternal records and autopsy findings are reviewed monthly by a stillbirth committee composed of obstetricians, maternal-fetal medicine specialists, neonatologists, geneticists, and perinatal pathologists.

137
00:18:18,985 --> 00:18:23,585
If possible, the cause of death is assigned from available evidence.

138
00:18:23,585 --> 00:18:34,335
Most importantly, parents are then contacted and offered counseling regarding the cause of death, the potential recurrence risk, and possible strategies to avoid recurrence .

139
00:18:34,335 --> 00:18:36,285
Perinatal Care.

140
00:18:36,285 --> 00:18:40,810
Fetal death is psychologically traumatic for a woman and her family.

141
00:18:40,810 --> 00:19:00,047
Further stressors include an interval of more than 24 hours between the diagnosis of fetal death and labor induction; not seeing her neonate for as long as she desires; having no tokens of remembrance; and poor communication with caregivers (Heazell, 2016; Siassakos, 2018).

142
00:19:00,047 --> 00:19:05,247
Seeing and holding a stillborn neonate assists parental psychological well-being .

143
00:19:05,247 --> 00:19:10,197
Unfortunately, few obstetrical providers receive formal training in

144
00:19:10,348 --> 00:19:12,785
perinatal bereavement care .

145
00:19:12,735 --> 00:19:19,823
At Parkland Hospital, care is coordinated through a dedicated nursing team affiliated with labor and delivery.

146
00:19:19,823 --> 00:19:28,673
Intervention includes time with the neonate, keepsake items, photographs, chaplaincy consultation, and bereavement support information.

147
00:19:28,673 --> 00:19:34,673
These principles dovetail with the recent global consensus recommendations for bereavement care .

148
00:19:34,673 --> 00:19:43,948
A woman experiencing a stillbirth or early miscarriage is at increased risk for depression and posttraumatic stress disorder and should be closely monitored .

149
00:19:43,948 --> 00:19:51,435
Distinct from stillbirth, fetuses with life-limiting conditions and short survival also merit special consideration.

150
00:19:51,435 --> 00:20:00,623
Conceptually, perinatal palliative care provides obstetrical and neonatal care to maximize the comfort and quality of life for these newborns .

151
00:20:00,623 --> 00:20:10,360
Components of this care include review of the diagnosis, creation of a birth plan, bereavement counseling, and consultation with neonatal services.

152
00:20:10,360 --> 00:20:17,710
This care is typically provided by a team that is able to offer medical, psychological, and spiritual support.

153
00:20:17,710 --> 00:20:22,748
During planning, parents’ values, goals, and fears are a focus.

154
00:20:22,748 --> 00:20:24,760
Prior Stillbirth.

155
00:20:24,760 --> 00:20:31,410
The woman with a prior stillbirth has a substantial risk for a recurrence (see Table 35-2.

156
00:20:31,410 --> 00:20:36,235
Preterm or growth-restricted stillborn fetuses magnify this risk .

157
00:20:36,235 --> 00:20:43,885
In one cohort of 43 women with a recurrent stillbirth, most fetuses died before 28 weeks’ gestation .

158
00:20:43,885 --> 00:20:46,548
Figure 35-4.

159
00:20:46,548 --> 00:20:53,023
Stillbirth Collaborative Research Network: gestational age distribution of recurrent stillbirths.

160
00:20:53,023 --> 00:20:58,998
An outlined approach for women with a prior stillbirth is shown in Table 35-3.

161
00:20:58,998 --> 00:21:07,485
Importantly, these recommendations are based primarily on limited or inconsistent scientific evidence or on expert opinions.

162
00:21:07,485 --> 00:21:11,010
Few studies address management of affected women .

163
00:21:11,010 --> 00:21:13,673
Table 35-3.

164
00:21:13,673 --> 00:21:20,473
Management of Subsequent Pregnancy after Stillbirth Very few risk factors for stillbirth are modifiable .

165
00:21:20,473 --> 00:21:26,898
Intuitively some such as hypertension or diabetes warrant disease-specific prevention strategies.

166
00:21:26,898 --> 00:21:35,223
Women with a prior fetal death due to placental insufficiency also are at increased risk for subsequent adverse perinatal outcomes .

167
00:21:35,223 --> 00:21:46,948
According to Reddy (2007), almost half of fetal deaths are associated with growth restriction, and thus sonographic anatomical assessment beginning at midpregnancy is recommended.

168
00:21:46,948 --> 00:21:51,673
This is followed by serial growth studies beginning at 28 weeks.

169
00:21:51,673 --> 00:22:01,348
Detection of growth restriction may lead to iatrogenic prematurity and its associated longterm childhood consequences but to fewer intrauterine deaths .

170
00:22:01,348 --> 00:22:10,998
Weeks and associates (1995) evaluated fetal biophysical testing in 300 women whose only indication was prior stillbirth.

171
00:22:10,998 --> 00:22:18,348
There was one subsequent stillbirth, and only three fetuses had abnormal testing results before 32 weeks.

172
00:22:18,348 --> 00:22:28,885
Notably, the gestational age of the prior stillborn fetus did not relate to the incidence or timing of abnormal test results or fetal jeopardy in the subsequent pregnancy.

173
00:22:28,885 --> 00:22:38,885
These investigators concluded that antepartum surveillance should begin at 32 weeks’ gestation or later in the otherwise healthy woman with a history of stillbirth.

174
00:22:38,885 --> 00:22:51,848
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2020) support this, with the caveat that it increases the iatrogenic preterm delivery rate.

175
00:22:51,848 --> 00:23:03,798
Although fetal movement counting strategies are routinely employed and described in Chapter 20 (p. 384), few data guide its use in clinical practice for those with a prior stillbirth .

176
00:23:03,798 --> 00:23:10,098
Unless other indications intervene, delivery at 39 weeks’ gestation is recommended.

177
00:23:10,098 --> 00:23:17,373
This timing minimizes fetal mortality rates, although the degree of risk reduction may be greater for older women .

178
00:23:17,373 --> 00:23:24,360
Labor induction is suitable, and cesarean delivery is reserved for those with a contraindication to induction.

179
00:23:24,360 --> 00:23:26,973
Changes In Stillbirth Rates.

180
00:23:26,973 --> 00:23:34,273
As noted, following declines through 2011, the United States fetal mortality rate has changed little .

181
00:23:34,273 --> 00:23:40,398
Interpretation of these rates in the context of changing healthcare strategies has spawned debate.

182
00:23:40,398 --> 00:23:46,348
One example is the trend against nonmedically indicated deliveries before 39 weeks’

183
00:23:46,516 --> 00:23:50,878
gestation and its subsequent effect on term stillbirth rates .

184
00:23:50,828 --> 00:23:57,791
This practice’s value for neonatal outcome is described in Chapter 26 (p. 486).

185
00:23:57,791 --> 00:24:08,053
To analyze whether implementation of this 39-week rule has altered the term stillbirth rate, data from 45 states and the District of Columbia were examined .

186
00:24:08,053 --> 00:24:17,503
The proportion of births before 39 weeks progressively declined from 2007 and 2013, but the term stillbirth rate increased.

187
00:24:17,503 --> 00:24:22,316
This suggests that the 39-week rule may cause unintended harm.

188
00:24:22,316 --> 00:24:36,141
One review of studies with 15 million pregnancies showed that the prospective risk of stillbirth rose from 0.11 per 1000 births at 37 weeks to 3.18 per 1000 births at 42 weeks .

189
00:24:36,141 --> 00:24:47,466
MacDorman and associates (2015b) also evaluated trends in stillbirth rates by gestational ages in the United States between 2006 and 2012.

190
00:24:47,466 --> 00:24:57,453
They used a “traditional stillbirth rate,” which was calculated using a denominator composed of the live-birth number plus the stillbirth number at a given gestational age.

191
00:24:57,453 --> 00:25:04,878
They found increased rates at 24 to 27, 34 to 36, and 38 weeks’ gestation.

192
00:25:04,878 --> 00:25:09,903
Alternatively, no differences were found in “prospective stillbirth rates.

193
00:25:09,903 --> 00:25:19,216
These rates were calculated using a denominator composed of the number of women who are pregnant at a given gestational age for weeks 21 through 42.

194
00:25:19,216 --> 00:25:28,028
The discrepancies in stillbirth rates appear to primarily derive from the decline in the birth numbers in the preterm and early-term gestational ages.

195
00:25:28,028 --> 00:25:43,341
In sum, implementation of the 39-week rule has reduced the number of elective births before 39 weeks’ gestation, although an unintended consequence may be an increase in term stillbirths—especially among women with medical complications.

196
00:25:43,341 --> 00:25:53,366
The importance of induction before 39 weeks in pregnant women with complications to prevent stillbirth is underscored by Little and colleagues (2015).

197
00:25:53,366 --> 00:26:05,491
These authors performed a retrospective multistate analysis of early-term deliveries—370/7 to 386/7 weeks—from 2005 to 2011.

198
00:26:05,491 --> 00:26:13,078
They noted a decline in the number of early-term deliveries during this time but not a significant change in the term stillbirth rates.

199
00:26:13,078 --> 00:26:26,403
There was, however, a 25-percent rise in the rate of term, singleton stillbirths among women with diabetes, and this was attributed to clinicians misapplying early-term delivery policies to high-risk women.

200
00:26:26,403 --> 00:26:34,603
Undoubtedly, continued surveillance of stillbirth rates is warranted for both high- and lowrisk pregnancies at the state and national level.

